These files are for changing the key layout of my Razer Tartarus v2 and Kensington Expert Trackball, using HWDB.
They should go on the /etc/udev/hwdb.d/ directory.

to out what event file your device is, use:
	sudo libinput debug-events --show-keycodes

Take note of the `eventXX` part of the output. it should be the first thing to get printed when you press a key.

to find out information about a device, use this (replace `event4` with the event corresponding to your device):
	udevadm info --attribute-walk --path=$(udevadm info --query=path --name=/dev/input/event4)
Look for the part that lists lines starting with ATTRS{xxx}. Take note of the id/bustype, id/product, and id/vendor
Now you construct the line in the .hwdb file that matches with the device, write something like the following (make sure to use uppercase letters to write this hexadecimal number):
	evdev:input:b0003v1532p022B*
	            |    |    |
	            |    |    `- This is the id/product
	            |    `- This is the id/vendor
	            `- This is the id/bustype

to get the number for remapping a specific key on the device, use:
	evtest
you will be prompted to enter a number. this corresponds to the same event from before. Now you should press a key and look at the number after "value":
Event: time 1790141969.431782, type 4 (EV_MSC), code 4 (MSC_SCAN), value 70015 <--- THIS ONE!
Event: time 1790141969.431782, type 1 (EV_KEY), code 19 (KEY_R), value 0
Event: time 1790141969.431782, -------------- SYN_REPORT ------------

That's the number you will use to write the mappings e.g. KEYBOARD_KEY_70015


---

After making changes to these files, run the following commands in a root shell
	systemd-hwdb update
	udevadm trigger

...or execute as root the shellscript that i left in this directory (currently only does the tartarus)